1ho6|n|A word, phrase, or sentence, by way of comment or explanation, inserted in, or attached to, a sentence which would be grammatically complete without it. It is usually inclosed within curved lines (see def. 2 below), or dashes. ; One of the curved lines () which inclose a parenthetic word or phrase.	a:16kf,66,2~16kf,66,2	v:16kf,66,2~16kf,66,2
1hpu|n|A piece of ground inclosed, and stored with beasts of the chase, which a man may have by prescription, or the king's grant. ; A tract of ground kept in its natural state, about or adjacent to a residence, as for the preservation of game, for walking, riding, or the like. ; A piece of ground, in or near a city or town, inclosed and kept for ornament and recreation; as, Hyde Park in London; Central Park in New York. ; A space occupied by the animals, wagons, pontoons, and materials of all kinds, as ammunition, ordnance stores, hospital stores, provisions, etc., when brought together; also, the objects themselves; as, a park of wagons; a park of artillery. ; A partially inclosed basin in which oysters are grown.	a:1xx1,95,1~1xx1,95,1	N:1lqt,52,2~1lqt,52,2
